<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="(-)-medicarpin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>Pterocarpan phytoalexins are antimicrobial compounds in leguminous plants formed upon pathogenic attacks and similar biotic stress-related events. |FRAME: Medicarpin Medicarpin|, first isolated from heartwood of the tropical tree |FRAME: TAX-149635| |CITS: [Harper65]|, is a major pterocarpan phytoalexin. It has been reported from many legumes, including alfalfa (|FRAME: TAX-3879|) |CITS: [Smith71][8071365]|, red clover (|FRAME: TAX-57577|) |CITS: [Higgins72]|, broad bean (|FRAME: TAX-3906|) |CITS: [Hargreaves76]|, all members of the |FRAME: TAX-78532| genus |CITS: [Ingham76]|, and the model legume |FRAME: TAX-3880| |CITS: [15605242]|. |FRAME: Medicarpin Medicarpin| is found in both a (-) and a (+) conformations. While |FRAME: CPD-3402| appears to be the most common form, |FRAME: CPD-20031| has been reported from several plants such as |FRAME: TAX-3897| and |FRAME: TAX-77268| |CITS: [Vanetten83][Martínez-Sotres12]|. The biosynthetic route of |FRAME: CPD-3402| starts with |FRAME: FORMONONETIN|, a 4&apos;-methoxylated isoflavone, which is also the origin for the pterocarpans |FRAME: CPD-3631| in chickpea and |FRAME: CPD-3944| in pea. All pterocarpans are easily induced and show time- and tissue-specific expression patterns |CITS: [7866024]| |CITS: [Daniel90]|. Malonate glucosides of pterocarpans frequently accumulate constitutively in below-ground organs of legumes, indicating that under certain physiological circumstances a metabolic link exists between isoflavone conjugation reactions and pterocarpan synthesis |CITS: [Daniel90][Weidemann91][Barz92]|. In leguminous plants pterocarpans have been found with different stereochemical orientation. Chick pea and alfalfa accumulate only (-)- or (6aR,11aR)-pterocarpans, whereas peanuts accumulate pterocarpans of the opposite stereochemistry (+)- (6aS, 11aS). Some legumes (e.g. the scholar tree, &lt;i&gt;Sophora japonica&lt;/i&gt;) build up an racemic mixture of both (-)- and (+)-pterocarpans. The stereochemistry of this phytoalexins can be crucial since it has been found that pathogens cannot break down the opposite enantiomer of the pterocarpans usually found in their target plants |CITS: [VanEtten83]|. A study of the last enzyme of the pathway, |FRAME: EC-4.2.1.139|, from |FRAME: TAX-46348| has shown that the stereochemistry of the final product is determined by the chiral center at C-3. A (3&lt;i&gt;R&lt;/i&gt;) configuration results in production of a (-) pterocarpan, while a (3&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;) configuration produces a (+)-pterocarpan. However, it is still not known at what step the conformation of the C-3 carbon is modified and by which enzyme |CITS: [28394400]|.</Description>
